\section{Dependence as a Region Interface}

Weise, Crew, Ernst, and Steensgaard's \textit{Value Dependence Graphs: Representation without Taxation} replaces extraneous sequencing in a CFG by dependence on computed values. Stanier and Watson's survey notes a difficulty: a demand-based representation can discard an undemanded computation that used to prevent the program from terminating. State dependence accounts for essential order not otherwise visible through result values. Reissmann, Meyer, Bahmann, and Sj\"alander's \textit{RVSDG: An Intermediate Representation for Optimizing Compilers} adds a further structural discipline: primitive computations and compound nodes inhabit nested regions with explicit interfaces.

Inside a region, each operation's input is wired to exactly one origin of the matching type, either a region argument or the output of another node in that region. Region results obey the same rule. A compound node maps its inputs and outputs to the arguments and results of its subregions; an arbitrary edge does not pierce a region boundary. Edges can stand for data or state, and the graph of dependencies within each region is acyclic. This is stronger than putting cycles for loops into a flat graph: repetition is part of a node's meaning, not part of the ordinary graph evaluation rule.

\section{Worked Example: A Zero-Trip Loop}

A \(\gamma\)-node chooses and evaluates one of several regions with matching signatures. A \(\theta\)-node evaluates its body at least once. The body yields a continuation predicate and a tuple that either feeds the next iteration or supplies the final outputs. To append \(0,1,\ldots,n-1\) to an external log when \(n>0\), combine both constructs. Let \(S\) be the log state, assume \(n\) fits the chosen integer type, and let \texttt{emit} consume and produce log state.

\begin{verbatim}
gamma(n > 0; n, S):
  false(n, S): yield S
  true(n, S):
    (i_out, bound_out, S_out) = theta(0, n, S):
      body(i, bound, s):
        s1 = emit(s, i)
        j  = i + 1
        yield (j < bound; j, bound, s1)
    yield S_out
\end{verbatim}

Initially the carried index is zero and the incoming log has no appended entries from this loop. At the beginning of iteration \(i\), the carried state has appended exactly the prefix of length \(i\). The body emits \(i\), then tests \(j=i+1\); the invariant proves by induction that the true region returns precisely the log with \(n\) appended entries. The false region forwards the original state. Dropping the \(\gamma\)-guard would emit zero even for \(n=0\). Dropping the state output of the body would lose the very dependence through which the externally visible result is demanded.

The nested interfaces in Figure~\ref{fig:visual-196} show how the false region bypasses even the first execution of the loop body.

