\section{Remove a Loop Port Only After Closing Its Back-Edge Demands}

A loop output with no external users can still be needed to supply the next iteration. In an illustrative RVSDG-style simplifier, each carried index has four corresponding ports: an initial input, a body argument, a body result, and a loop output. Assume that body dependencies are available in the graph, and that any computation whose removal would change observable effects, traps, or termination has an explicit dependency from a retained state result, or has been marked as an independent root. Given the loop and its live external outputs, the pass finds carried indices to retain and projects all four port lists in the same order. It does not change the loop's continuation predicate.

\begin{verbatim}
live_carried(loop, used_outputs):
  keep = copy(used_outputs)
  work = [loop.predicate] + loop.observable_roots
  append body_results_at(keep) to work
  seen = empty set
  while work is not empty:
    v = pop work
    if v in seen: continue
    add v to seen
    if v is BodyArg(i):
      # A live argument needs its value on the next iteration.
      add i to keep
      push loop.body_result[i] onto work
    else: push local_dependencies(v) onto work
  # Project all related ports with one common index map.
  return project_ports(loop, keep in original order)
\end{verbatim}

Take a loop carrying \((i,total,spare)\) initially \((0,0,100)\). The body computes \(j=i+1\), continues while \(j<3\), and returns \((j,total+i,spare+7)\), with only the final total used. The predicate and total both demand \(i\); the recurrence for total demands its old argument. The worklist retains \(i,total\) and drops \texttt{spare}. Both versions execute three iterations and return \(3\), from successive totals \(0,1,3\). Dropping \(i\) just because its final output is unused would cut a real back-edge dependency.

The invariant is closure under feedback: every retained body-argument demand has a matching retained result and initial input. With visited marks, the traversal is linear in the reachable dependence edges and ports, despite the implicit feedback. Initial input producers must still be retained when their computation is observable even if the loop no longer needs their values.
